cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "LiouScope: Multi-Diagnostic Relaxation Analysis for Open Quantum Lattice Systems"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - name: "Coworker Research"
version: "0.5.0"
date-released: "2026-06-25"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21246109
# doi above = immutable *version* DOI for released v0.5.0. Directly verified against
# the Zenodo record on 2026-07-08: title "LiouScope: Multi-Diagnostic Relaxation
# Analysis for Open Quantum Lattice Systems", version v0.5.0, Coworker Research,
# published 2026-07-07 (Zenodo). The concept DOI (spanning all versions) is to be
# recorded separately once independently verified. Note: `date-released` above is
# the software release date of tag v0.5.0; the Zenodo deposit date (2026-07-07)
# differs and is documented in the release audit, not conflated here.
# This citation describes released v0.5.0; VCS main uses a PEP 440 development
# version until the next release is cut.
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/marcohost33-maker/Liouscope"
url: "https://github.com/marcohost33-maker/Liouscope"
abstract: >-
  LiouScope is an open-source diagnostic framework for time-homogeneous
  Markovian open quantum systems described by Gorini-Kossakowski-Sudarshan-
  Lindblad (GKSL) generators. It quantifies when and why the Liouvillian gap
  fails as a relaxation-time predictor. The current code-backed public surface
  computes D1-D20, plus sub-diagnostics D2b/D7b/D11b, and the opt-in D24 Zhou
  mixing-time predictor; D21-D23 are schema-defined reserved slots, not
  implemented diagnostics in this repository. Mechanism labels use the
  A1-A12-v3.1 taxonomy.
# CONVENTION (added 2026-08-09): `abstract` describes the surface of the CITED
# release only -- the one named by `version` and the immutable version DOI above.
# Capability sentences for work that is merged but not yet released do NOT belong
# here; they would credit the published artifact with results it cannot produce.
# Record them in CHANGELOG.md under [Unreleased] and move them into this abstract
# when the release is cut. Pending for the next cut: concurrently supported
# mechanism hypotheses are reported alongside the dominant label with their
# priority-shadowing status (`ClassificationResult.triggered_hypotheses`,
# issue #102), and the support score is a documented deterministic heuristic
# rather than a calibrated probability; a hypothesis-wise evidence matrix
# spanning the full A1-A12 taxonomy reports supporting measurements,
# counterevidence, missing evidence and explicit fail-closed claim floors, with
# the schema-reserved classes excluded from claims and coverage denominators
# (`ClassificationResult.hypothesis_matrix`, issue #102); and the zero-mode
# separation underlying D1/D3/D4/D9/D16/D19/D20/D24 is scale-relative, so those
# diagnostics no longer depend on the choice of rate unit (issue #108). The
# last item CHANGES NUMERICAL RESULTS for generators whose spectral radius is
# far from unity and must be described as a correction, not merely an addition,
# when the release is cut. It must NOT be described as making the mechanism
# verdict unit-invariant in general: the A10/F5 branch still gates on the
# rate-dimensioned henrici_eta threshold (open in #101).
# Also pending for the next cut: the spectral layer verifies the computed
# spectrum against the exact identity vec(I)^H L = 0 before reading D1/D3/D4
# off it, and re-solves by an alternative LAPACK route when a stiff generator
# makes zgeev deflate the zero mode away (issue #112). This too CHANGES
# NUMERICAL RESULTS -- on stiff generators only -- and must be described as a
# correction rather than an addition: the previously reported gap could be a
# spurious mode rather than an inaccurate one (measured 7.28e-6 against a true
# 1.074e-5 on a four-level classical jump network). It must NOT be described as
# making the dense spectral layer reliable for arbitrarily stiff generators:
# when no route satisfies the certificate the layer is marked unresolved, which
# is a disclosure, not a repair. Certification and downstream zero-mode
# filtering share ONE operator-derived scale (rtol * eps * ||L||_2) at every
# call site that holds the operator -- spectral layer, Mpemba layer, Petermann
# factors and the D24 predictor, which is now itself routed through the
# certified eigensolve and returns an unconverged record on unresolved spectra
# (round-13 review). This corrects spurious near-zero or negative D1 gaps on
# strongly non-normal generators, where the spectral radius understates the
# eigensolver backward error by the ||L||_2 / max|lambda| ratio (measured
# 3.9e3 at dimension four). The operator-derived bound applies only when the
# certificate is applicable (trace preservation established); otherwise the
# filters fall back to the radius-based tolerance, and the Petermann factors
# (D9) likewise consume the certified eigendecomposition, withholding NaN on
# unresolved spectra (round-14 review). The eigenvector-producing certificate
# additionally validates per-mode left/right eigenvector residuals before
# certifying (a small |lambda| does not vouch for the vectors D19/D9 consume),
# and the repair ladder continues past ambiguous candidates so a later route
# can resolve what the incumbent cannot (round-15 review). The Prony fallback
# seeds for the M3b fit are grid-relative (round-16 review), removing a
# rate-unit dependence that corrupted fitted rates on non-uniform grids far
# from unit span; D24's partial recomputation and D11's eigenvalue input now
# match the certificate of what they actually consume. Hermiticity
# validation of the Hamiltonian is
# likewise scale-relative rather than absolute (issue #109), which closes a
# fail-open path that admitted non-GKSL generators at small ||H||; this changes
# which inputs are ACCEPTED, not the numerics of accepted ones.
# Also pending for the next cut: the DEFAULT relaxation time grid spans a fixed
# number of e-foldings of the slowest mode, [0, 10 / Delta], instead of the
# absolute window [0, 10] (PR #115). This CHANGES NUMERICAL RESULTS -- for every
# run that does not pass an explicit t_grid and whose gap is not Delta = 1 --
# and must be described as a correction, not an addition: the previously
# reported rates were measured on a window whose relation to the dynamics
# depended on the caller's unit of time, so on an amplitude-damped qubit under
# the pure rescale L -> cL the fitted beta_D drifted 22%, the D17 linear rate
# was wrong by a factor ~430 at c = 1e-6, and four different A-classes were
# reported for one system. It must NOT be described as making the fitted
# relaxation rates invariant under an arbitrary change of rate units: the
# least-squares solver's own convergence controls remain unit-dependent (open in
# #111), and the mechanism verdict still gates on the rate-dimensioned
# henrici_eta threshold (open in #101). The claim is bounded to what was
# measured -- beta_D and beta_D_linear track the rescaling to <=1.2e-3 relative
# over c in [1e-6, 1e6] with a stable AICc winner, and the A-class is stable
# over c in [1e-6, 1] -- on the two qubit systems tested.
# Also pending for the next cut: on a system whose rates are separated by more
# than about eightfold the default window is now TWO-SCALE rather than uniform,
# and the GLS residual model switches with it from discrete AR(1) to
# continuous-time CAR(1) (per-step exp(-theta dt_k)). This CHANGES NUMERICAL
# RESULTS for exactly those runs and is a correction, not an addition: on two
# independent damped qubits with rates 1e-6 and 1 the uniform window's winning
# M2 fit reported a second rate of 2.17e-05 for a component that was never
# sampled (true value 1), and the D17 linear rate sat 0.58 relative from the
# certified gap; the two-scale window recovers 1.10 and 0.021 respectively.
# It must NOT be described as resolving arbitrary multiscale dynamics: the two
# segments resolve the FASTEST and the SLOWEST mode, and an intermediate
# timescale can still fall between the coarse late samples -- measured 0.0019
# samples per e-folding for the middle mode of a 1e-6 / 1e-3 / 1 system, where
# the UnderResolvedTransientWarning correctly still fires. Nor is the
# small-sample bias correction of the AR(1) path carried over to CAR(1): every
# way of anchoring it to a single step of a multi-decade grid was measured to
# destroy the estimator's discrimination, so it is disclosed rather than
# applied.
# Also pending for the next cut: where the zero-mode certificate is applicable
# but NOT resolved, the spectral layer now withholds D3 (oscillating_gap) and
# D4 (spectral_spread) as NaN and reports has_complex_pairs as None, under the
# same predicate that already withheld D1 (round-17 review, PR #127). This
# CHANGES REPORTED RESULTS for exactly those runs and is a correction, not an
# addition: the three values were previously read off the candidate spectrum
# that the layer's own warning declares unreliable (measured on the issue-#113
# stiff fixture: D3 = 0.0 and has_complex_pairs = False, i.e. an assertion that
# no oscillatory separation exists, taken from a spectrum whose slow modes lie
# below the eigensolver's backward error). Consumers see the A8 oscillatory
# rung become UNEVALUABLE instead of NOT_SUPPORTED for those runs. It must NOT
# be described as making the spectral layer reliable on stiff generators: the
# unresolved case is still unresolved, only no longer reported as measured.
# D2/D2b are unaffected -- they are computed from the operator and the steady
# state rather than from the candidate spectrum.
# Also pending for the next cut, non-uniform grids only (PR #127 review): CAR(1)
# theta maximises the stationary likelihood reported to AICc, every candidate
# counts correlation and variance, and AICc uses the observed point count instead
# of N_eff. This can change the selected model, its rates and the A-class on those
# grids. Uniform grids are untouched; the same change there is PR #157.
# Also pending for the next cut, all grids (PR #127 cross-family review
# LIO-02/LIO-03): the M0..M3b AICc winner must be resolved above the rounding
# level of the fitted curve and above where the optimiser stopped; otherwise a
# tie containing M0 selects M0 and any other tie reports "INDETERMINATE" (rate
# NaN). This CHANGES the selected model on curves fitted at float64 precision
# (validation systems V2 and V3, trace distance: M1/M3a -> M0) and must be
# described as a METHODOLOGY correction. It must NOT be described as a
# statistical indeterminacy rule: ties within ordinary sampling noise are still
# resolved by plain AICc. The default relaxation window without t_grid is
# gap-scaled and is not the v0.5.0 window (see CHANGELOG, migration note).
# Also pending for the next cut: the Gaussian likelihood behind AICc is
# evaluated in log-RSS space rather than by forming the residual sum of squares
# in ordinary units, and an exact-zero RSS is an explicit abstention rather than
# a floored variance (issue #135). This CHANGES NUMERICAL RESULTS and is a
# METHODOLOGY correction, not an addition: AICc ordering, the selected model
# M0..M3b and therefore the reported decay rate can differ for runs whose
# residual scale previously over- or underflowed, and a perfect fit now yields
# no confidence interval instead of a zero-width one. The likelihood value is
# scale-covariant and likelihood DIFFERENCES between models are invariant under
# a common rescaling of the residuals, which is the quantity AICc consumes. It
# must NOT be described as making model selection scale-free in general: only
# the likelihood path was corrected, and the residual-scale parameter itself
# still has a float64 representation limit -- a fit whose MLE scale underflows
# keeps its log-space likelihood and stays selectable, but its bootstrap
# interval is withheld (round-1 review of PR #147). Archived analyses whose
# selected model matters should be re-run; per-model likelihoods are not run-
# manifest fields, so this change is not visible in input_hash.
# Also pending for the next cut: the Hermiticity validation of the Hamiltonian
# measures its defect against the scale of the COHERENT component alone, read
# in the canonical Lindblad gauge (PR #127, E3 settled by cross-family review
# 2026-09-12): gauge-fixed max|H0| after the compensation H0 = H + sum_k
# gamma_k (m_k L_k^dag - conj(m_k) L_k) / 2i that makes the jump operators
# traceless, with the dissipation scale max|sum_k gamma_k L0_k^dag L0_k| / 2
# reported for diagnostics only. Hermiticity is a structural property of H, so
# physical dissipation of any strength does NOT enlarge the tolerance: a
# numerically pure-gauge Hamiltonian such as I + 2**-53 e01 is refused with
# or without a dissipator, exactly as an identity-dominated H such as
# [[1e308, 1], [0, 1e308]], a null dissipator c*I and the Lindblad gauge
# L -> L + c*I are. As with issue #109 this changes which inputs are ACCEPTED,
# not the numerics of accepted ones. Without jump operators the verdict is
# main's gauge-fixed one; an H whose gauge-fixed scale is not finite is
# refused. The generator-relative reference max(coherent, dissipation) that an
# earlier state of PR #127 documented here never shipped in a release.
# Also pending for the next cut: trace-preserving generators can be restricted
# to the traceless operator space by an exact structural identity rather than by
# an eigenvalue-magnitude threshold (issue #113). For column-stacked operators
# trace preservation IS the statement vec(I)^H L = 0, so ker(vec(I)^H) -- the
# traceless space -- is invariant under L and carries every eigenvector with
# non-zero eigenvalue; the reduction L0 = B^H L B onto an explicit orthonormal
# basis B of that kernel therefore removes the unique stationary direction with
# no tolerance in the construction, and a degenerate stationary manifold keeps
# its additional traceless zero modes instead of having them filtered away.
# Admission is gated on BOTH readings of the identity -- the normwise ratio and
# the componentwise backward error of each column's trace equation (issue #130)
# -- because the normwise ratio alone is diluted to round-off by entries that
# take part in no violated trace equation, and it then admits generators whose
# traceless subspace is not invariant. This entry describes a METHODOLOGY that
# is available in the codebase, and it must NOT be described as changing any
# reported number: no diagnostic consumes the restriction yet, so D1-D24 are
# byte-identical to before and the run manifest contract is untouched
# (MANIFEST_SCHEMA is deliberately unchanged). When a diagnostic is routed
# through it, THAT change is the one that must be described as a correction.
# Also pending for the next cut: the trace-preservation defect vec(I)^H L is
# accumulated with exact per-column summation, falling back on overflow to an
# exact fixed-point accumulator in units of 2**-1074 that rounds once (#151; it
# replaced an exponent-banded fallback that could only abstain, reporting nan,
# on a residual surviving across its band boundary), instead of an ordinary
# matrix product (#139). This CHANGES A REPORTED
# NUMBER and is a correction, not an addition: the ordinary product could leave
# the float64 range on the way to a column sum that is exactly zero -- measured
# for d=16 with eight coefficients +2.5e307 and eight -2.5e307 in one column,
# where it returned inf+nanj and a legal, fully representable generator was
# refused -- and where it did not overflow it returned whatever its summation
# order produced rather than the exact sum of the represented coefficients. The
# reported `trace_defect`, which travels into persisted reports through the
# zero-mode certificate, is now the correctly rounded exact sum: for the stiff
# four-level fixture it reads 1.158640e-11 instead of 0.0, adjudicated with
# exact rational arithmetic. Certificate VERDICTS do not move at any sane
# tolerance, because the affected readings are at rounding level (2.29e-17
# relative for that fixture). It must NOT be described as making the layer
# overflow-proof in general: only the trace-equation accumulation was changed.
# MANIFEST_SCHEMA is deliberately unchanged -- `trace_defect` is a report
# field, not a run-manifest field, so the manifest contract is untouched and
# `input_hash` does not move. The #151 fallback is reached only by columns whose
# ordinary exact summation overflows; physical generators do not reach it, so
# it moves no reported number of theirs.
# Also pending for the next cut: the traceless restriction ENFORCES its own
# audit numbers as a postcondition (#150) instead of only reporting them. With
# B an orthonormal basis of ker(q^H), ||q^H L B|| <= ||q^H L|| (a projection)
# and L B - B (B^H L B) = q (q^H L B) (because I - B B^H = q q^H), so the
# invariance and reconstruction defects are fixed by the admitted trace defect
# up to rounding; the restriction fails closed when either exceeds that by more
# than reduction_rtol * (eps * ||L||_F + d**3 * 2**-1074), reduction_rtol
# defaulting to the existing ZERO_MODE_EPS_FACTOR. Like the restriction itself
# this moves no reported number: no diagnostic consumes it yet.
# Also pending for the next cut: the spectral layer records AUDIT-ONLY
# eigenvalue-conditioning evidence for the stationary mode (issue #117). The
# reciprocal condition number of the zero set -- sigma_min(Y^H X) over
# orthonormal bases of the left and right invariant subspaces, which reduces to
# |y^H x| for a simple eigenvalue -- is reported alongside the left/right
# residuals, the separation, the trace-preservation defect and the first-order
# forward-error estimates those imply. It exists because a backward error does
# NOT bound the forward eigenvalue displacement for a non-normal generator:
# measured on the 4x4 (two-level, d = 2) fixture of the 2026-09-11 external
# review, a 1.00e-14 perturbation moves the stationary eigenvalue to 1.00e-07,
# which the certificate band (4.44e-13) under-predicts by 2.25e5x and the
# conditioning-scaled estimate (5.00e-08) reproduces within a factor of two.
# (The canonical FOUR-level network referenced elsewhere in this file is a
# different, 16x16 fixture; the two must not be conflated.) This entry
# describes a MEASUREMENT that is available in the codebase and it must NOT be
# described as changing any reported number: no filter, gap, certificate,
# verdict or tier consumes it, D1-D24 are byte-identical and the run manifest
# contract is untouched (MANIFEST_SCHEMA deliberately unchanged; the evidence is
# a report field, so `input_hash` does not move). It must also NOT be described
# as detecting stiff eigensolver failure: on the canonical #112 network the
# deflated, WRONG spectrum carries condition numbers between 1 and 25, so
# conditioning is additive to the structural certificate and no substitute for
# it. Issue #168 corrects the repeated/defective-cluster measurement itself:
# a multi-mode zero cluster is reordered in a complex Schur form, the Sylvester equation
# T11 R - R T22 = T12 is solved, and the reported cluster figure is the actual
# reciprocal spectral-projector 2-norm 1/sqrt(1 + ||R||_2^2). LAPACK xTRSEN.S
# is NOT identified with that exact value; it is only the documented
# Frobenius-norm lower bound. A multi-mode zero cluster is now explicitly
# CLUSTER_ONLY: no scalar first-order eigenvalue-displacement estimate is
# claimed, because defective multiplicity makes such a scalar condition
# non-identifiable from numerical proximity alone. Before any multi-mode
# projector is reported, the independent Schur spectrum must match the report's
# spectrum mode-for-mode inside the existing round-off agreement band; otherwise
# the audit withholds as schur_cluster_mismatch. Simple zero modes keep the
# scalar path. A call WITHOUT a zero_tolerance returns the verdict ABSTAIN
# (observations only, never BENIGN): without a cutoff the zero set is not
# defined in a basis-independent way (PR #173 review). The spectral layer always
# passes its certified cutoff, so no reported number moves. When a diagnostic is
# gated on this evidence, THAT change is the one that must be described as a
# correction. Issue #183 closes the audited group under the arithmetic's own
# resolution: two eigenvalues whose midpoint lies in the pseudospectrum at
# eps * ||L||_F count as unresolved, so a defective pair split by round-off is
# conditioned whole in every basis (CLUSTER_ONLY), while a genuinely split pair
# such as the 1e-14 fixture above stays a scalar CONDITIONING_LIMITED. The SVD is
# skipped only where the resolvent bound sigma_min(L - zI) >= 1/sum_j
# 1/(s_j |lambda_j - z|) (Embree & Trefethen 2001) proves the pair resolved, and
# the Schur agreement gate, when its strict mode-for-mode pairing fails, accepts
# agreement to that same resolution (still a bijection; a resolvably different
# spectrum is refused). Audit only; no reported number moves.
# Also pending for the next cut: relaxation trajectories for d >= 8 may be
# propagated by the Al-Mohy & Higham exponential action instead of a
# materialised expm(L t) (#162). The backend is chosen by a deterministic
# size/cost rule, recorded in RelaxationResult.trajectory_backend, and agrees
# with the dense reference to round-off (<= 2e-14 measured, 1e-11 declared);
# fitted rates then move only within the least-squares termination tolerance
# (xtol = 1e-8; <= 3e-8 relative measured). d < 8 (every anchor fixture) is
# unchanged bit-for-bit. It must be described as a numerical-method change,
# not as a change of any diagnostic's definition.
# Also pending for the next cut: D18 (initial-state sensitivity) may evolve its
# Haar block by the same exponential action instead of one materialised
# expm(L t) (#178 item 1). The backend is a deterministic function of
# (L, t, n_rhs) -- n = d**2 < 64 always dense, above that the action's counted
# products charged 1 + 8 / n_rhs against the dense estimate -- and is recorded
# in LepResult.initial_state_backend; condition (3.13) is scaled by 1/n_rhs so
# the global NumPy random state stays untouched. Every n < 64 system (every
# anchor fixture) is unchanged bit-for-bit; above that the two backends agree
# within the existing dense/action tolerance tests. It must be described as a
# numerical-method change, not as a change of D18's definition.
# Also pending for the next cut: for n = d**2 > 128 the D10, D10b, D11b and D13
# grid sweeps evaluate sigma_min(zI - L) from one shared complex Schur form by
# inverse Lanczos on the shifted triangle (Trefethen 1999; Wright & Trefethen
# 2001), with a dense SVD of the triangle for any point that does not converge,
# instead of a dense SVD or a capped SuperLU power iteration per point (#178
# item 2). Measured values agree with the dense formulas to <= 1.8e-13
# relative and the D13 membership decisions are identical; D10b and D11b no
# longer return capped lower bounds. n <= 128 (every anchor fixture) is
# unchanged bit-for-bit. It must be described as a numerical-method change,
# not as a change of any diagnostic's definition.
# Also pending for the next cut: the GLS AR(1) fit presents the optimiser with
# residuals divided by the curve's own scale max|y| (issue #124), so SciPy's
# termination no longer turns the seed rate into a "converged" estimate for a
# tiny-amplitude curve whose amplitude is not a fitted parameter; where that
# rescaled problem leaves float64, the iteration falls back, with a
# RuntimeWarning, to the unscaled residuals exactly as before (PR #134). The
# lag-1 autocorrelation behind the AR(1) whitening is formed from residuals
# normalised by an exact power of two, so it no longer underflows to its floor
# below ~1e-162 or overflows to NaN above ~1e154; where the old computation
# stayed in range it is bit-identical. This CHANGES NUMERICAL RESULTS and is a
# METHODOLOGY correction: fitted rates, rho, AICc and the selected model can
# differ for curves far from unit scale. It must NOT be described as making the
# fit amplitude-invariant in general: a FREE amplitude parameter is covered by
# the solver's Jacobian-based variable scaling (x_scale="jac") only between
# about 1e-150 and 1e100 (measured on M0), the fallback keeps the unscaled
# problem's own limits below that, and the variable scaling can move fitted
# values within the optimiser's tolerance at unit scale too. MANIFEST_SCHEMA
# is deliberately unchanged -- none of these quantities
# is a run-manifest field, so `input_hash` does not move.
# Also pending for the next cut (issue #116), a labelling CORRECTION and not an
# addition. The v0.5.0 README (not this abstract) described the
# relaxation-rate interval unconditionally as BCa, four times. On the default 80-point grid no acceleration
# is estimated (a = 0), so that interval is the bias-corrected (BC) percentile
# interval. BCa, with delete-one jackknife acceleration, applies only to grids
# of <= 60 points. Each run now records which estimator it computed
# (`RelaxationResult.interval_method`: "BC" | "BCa-delete1" | "unavailable").
# NO NUMBER CHANGES. It must NOT be described as making the interval BCa, or as
# calibrating its coverage under the serially correlated residuals the fit
# models: that is a separate, open coverage study (issue #116, stages 2-4).
keywords:
  - open quantum systems
  - Lindblad
  - Liouvillian gap
  - Mpemba effect
  - non-normal dynamics
  - pseudospectrum
  - quantum relaxation
  - spectral projector
  - transient amplification
  - trace distance contractivity
  - mechanism classification
  - competing hypotheses
